- name: epy_block_0
  id: epy_block
  parameters:
    _source_code: "\"\"\"\nber_align_hier.py  \u2014  BER Align Hier Block\n===========================================\n\
      A GNU Radio embedded Python block that:\n  1. Buffers CAL_SIZE bytes from both\
      \ TX and RX streams\n  2. Cross-correlates (once) to find the bit-lag between\
      \ them\n  3. Locks the lag, applies a delay to the leading stream\n  4. Computes\
      \ BER on a sliding window and emits it as a PMT message\n\nPorts\n-----\n  in0\
      \  : TX byte stream  (unpacked bits, 1 bit per byte, MSB in bit 0)\n  in1  :\
      \ RX byte stream  (same format, from demod)\n  out0 : message port \"ber_out\"\
      \  \u2014 PMT pair  (lag_bits, ber_float)\n\nUsage in GRC\n------------\n  -\
      \ Drop in as an \"Embedded Python Block\"\n  - Connect TX source \u2192 in0,\
      \ demod output \u2192 in1\n  - Connect \"ber_out\" message port to a Message\
      \ Debug or your own sink\n  - Tune CAL_SIZE and REPORT_EVERY to taste\n\nParameters\
      \ (edit at top of class)\n----------------------------------\n  CAL_SIZE   \
      \  : bytes per stream used for the one-shot lag calibration (default 4096)\n\
      \  REPORT_EVERY : emit a BER message every this many bytes after lock  (default\
      \ 1024)\n\"\"\"\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport pmt\nfrom gnuradio import gr\n\
      \n\nclass blk(gr.sync_block):\n    \"\"\"Align TX vs RX byte streams via cross-correlation,\
      \ then report BER.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, cal_size=4096, report_every=1024):\n\
      \        gr.sync_block.__init__(\n            self,\n            name=\"BER\
      \ Align\",\n            in_sig=[np.uint8, np.uint8],   # in0=TX, in1=RX\n  \
      \          out_sig=None,                   # no stream output \u2014 messages\
      \ only\n        )\n\n        # ---- tuneable parameters ----\n        self.CAL_SIZE\
      \    = int(cal_size)\n        self.REPORT_EVERY = int(report_every)\n\n    \
      \    # ---- internal state ----\n        self._tx_cal   = []          # calibration\
      \ buffers\n        self._rx_cal   = []\n        self._locked   = False     \
      \  # True once lag is found\n        self._lag      = 0           # positive\
      \ = RX leads TX (trim RX front)\n                                     # negative\
      \ = TX leads RX (trim TX front)\n        self._delay_buf = []         # holds\
      \ the \"early\" stream during the lag gap\n        self._errors   = 0\n    \
      \    self._total    = 0\n        self._since_report = 0\n\n        # message\
      \ output port\n        self.message_port_register_out(pmt.intern(\"ber_out\"\
      ))\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\
      \    # helpers\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\
      \n    def _to_bits(self, byte_arr):\n        \"\"\"Unpack uint8 array (1 bit/byte,\
      \ bit in LSB) \u2192 float \xB10.5 for correlation.\"\"\"\n        bits = (byte_arr\
      \ & 0x01).astype(np.float32)\n        return bits - 0.5   # zero-mean for clean\
      \ cross-correlation\n\n    def _find_lag(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Cross-correlate\
      \ TX and RX calibration buffers.\n        Returns lag in *bits* (positive =\
      \ RX is ahead of TX).\n        \"\"\"\n        tx = self._to_bits(np.array(self._tx_cal,\
      \ dtype=np.uint8))\n        rx = self._to_bits(np.array(self._rx_cal, dtype=np.uint8))\n\
      \n        # Full cross-correlation\n        # scipy fft method is faster for\
      \ large buffers, but numpy is fine here\n        corr = np.correlate(rx, tx,\
      \ mode='full')\n\n        # Peak index \u2192 lag\n        # corr[len(tx)-1]\
      \ corresponds to zero lag\n        peak_idx = int(np.argmax(corr))\n       \
      \ lag = peak_idx - (len(tx) - 1)\n\n        print(f\"[BER Align] Calibration\
      \ complete. Lag = {lag} bits \"\n              f\"({'RX leads' if lag > 0 else\
      \ 'TX leads' if lag < 0 else 'aligned'})\")\n        return lag\n\n    def _emit_ber(self,\
      \ lag):\n        ber = self._errors / self._total if self._total > 0 else 0.0\n\
      \        msg = pmt.cons(\n            pmt.from_long(int(lag)),       # car:\
      \ lag in bits\n            pmt.from_double(float(ber))    # cdr: BER\n     \
      \   )\n        self.message_port_pub(pmt.intern(\"ber_out\"), msg)\n       \
      \ print(f\"[BER Align] BER = {ber:.6f}  \"\n              f\"({self._errors}/{self._total}\
      \ errors,  lag={lag} bits)\")\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\
      \    # main work function\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n\
      \n    def work(self, input_items, output_items):\n        tx_in = input_items[0]\n\
      \        rx_in = input_items[1]\n        n     = len(tx_in)\n\n        # ----\
      \ Phase 1: Calibration ----------------------------------------\n        if\
      \ not self._locked:\n            self._tx_cal.extend(tx_in.tolist())\n     \
      \       self._rx_cal.extend(rx_in.tolist())\n\n            if len(self._tx_cal)\
      \ >= self.CAL_SIZE:\n                self._tx_cal = self._tx_cal[:self.CAL_SIZE]\n\
      \                self._rx_cal = self._rx_cal[:self.CAL_SIZE]\n             \
      \   self._lag    = self._find_lag()\n                self._locked = True\n\n\
      \                # Pre-fill the delay buffer so we can start comparing immediately.\n\
      \                # The stream that arrives FIRST needs to wait for the other\
      \ to catch up.\n                # lag > 0 \u2192 RX is ahead, skip first `lag`\
      \ RX bits\n                # lag < 0 \u2192 TX is ahead, skip first `|lag|`\
      \ TX bits\n                # (We just discard the offset bytes; they were calibration\
      \ data anyway.)\n\n            return n   # consume all input, emit nothing\
      \ during calibration\n\n        # ---- Phase 2: Locked \u2014 compare bit by\
      \ bit ------------------------\n        lag = self._lag\n\n        if lag >=\
      \ 0:\n            # RX arrived lag bits earlier \u2192 trim lag bits off the\
      \ front of RX\n            # We already consumed cal data so just align within\
      \ this work call\n            tx_bits = tx_in & 0x01\n            rx_bits =\
      \ rx_in & 0x01\n\n            # Apply lag offset within available samples\n\
      \            offset = min(lag, n)\n            tx_cmp = tx_bits[: n - offset]\n\
      \            rx_cmp = rx_bits[offset: n]\n        else:\n            # TX arrived\
      \ |lag| bits earlier \u2192 trim from TX\n            offset  = min(-lag, n)\n\
      \            tx_bits = tx_in & 0x01\n            rx_bits = rx_in & 0x01\n  \
      \          tx_cmp  = tx_bits[offset: n]\n            rx_cmp  = rx_bits[: n -\
      \ offset]\n\n        # Count bit errors\n        if len(tx_cmp) > 0 and len(rx_cmp)\
      \ > 0:\n            cmp_len = min(len(tx_cmp), len(rx_cmp))\n            self._errors\
      \ += int(np.sum(tx_cmp[:cmp_len] != rx_cmp[:cmp_len]))\n            self._total\
      \  += cmp_len\n            self._since_report += cmp_len\n\n        # Emit BER\
      \ message periodically\n        if self._since_report >= self.REPORT_EVERY:\n\
      \            self._emit_ber(self._lag)\n            self._since_report = 0\n\
      \n        return n\n"
    affinity: ''
    alias: ''
    cal_size: 4096*10
    comment: Forget about the BER calculations. Could you have two inputs -- byte
      stream 1 and byte stream 2, and output delay I should add? I will then add the
      delay to stream 2, and the use the delayed stream 2 and the original stream
      1 to calculate the BER (using the inbuilt BER block)
    maxoutbuf: '0'
    minoutbuf: '0'
    report_every: '1024'
  states:
    _io_cache: ('BER Align', 'blk', [('cal_size', '4096'), ('report_every', '1024')],
      [('0', 'byte', 1), ('1', 'byte', 1)], [('ber_out', 'message', 1)], 'Align TX
      vs RX byte streams via cross-correlation, then report BER.', [])
    bus_sink: false
    bus_source: false
    bus_structure: null
    coordinate: [512, 1064.0]
    rotation: 0
    state: disabled
